{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdY9dsqepYwnrRBoB93y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Jellyfish's Nikolas Albarran points to a investment all the major AI labs are making: forward-deployed engineering organizations, which embed AI consultants inside customer companies to help them get started. TechCrunch's article describes Anthropic's new reporting tools as a kind of FDE-lite, a lighter version of that hands-on help.\n\nThe comparison fits what the tools do. Based on internal analytics and employee surveys, the reports show a team's output over a chosen period, what it cost, and where its workflows broke down, and they include recommendations for improving results. An embedded consultant would typically be looking for the same things. Here the diagnosis comes from software that a manager can open without waiting for an engagement, which suggests it could reach customers who don't have a consultant on site. The article doesn't say how the recommendations compare in quality to human advice, or which customers will get which level of support, so that remains an open question.\n\nThe reason the labs care shows up in Anthropic's numbers. The financials it shared with investors this week include 900% net revenue retention, meaning the same customers spent nine times as much in Q3 2026 as in Q3 2025. Albarran argues that as customers put effort into setting up their tools, switching gets harder. Help that improves how a customer uses the product adds to that effort, and the customer's workflows end up shaped around it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":874,"cache_read_input_tokens":5376,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":874},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":